you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  how swift action to cut methane  pollution would rapidly reduce the rate  of global warming  while the science museum is facing  criticism for its controversial sponsor  of a new  climate exhibition and the indonesian  eco warrior turning central javas  mountains  green  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions we aim to take you to the  hearts of the climate  crisis explain the data driving the  changes that are already affecting us  but also show you just how far we've  come  now when we talk about global warming  there's usually a big focus on  one greenhouse gas in particular carbon  dioxide  but it's far from the only reason that  our planet is heating up  well our science correspondent thomas  moore is here with me so thomas tell us  more  well we're not going to keep global  warming below 1.5 degrees unless we  dramatically cut emissions of another  greenhouse gas  methane that's the conclusion of the  united nations environment program  in a new report it says methane is 10  times more powerful than carbon dioxide  at trapping heat in the atmosphere yet  levels have more than doubled  since pre-industrial times so where's it  all coming from  well the report says human activity  accounts for around 380 million  tons of methane being released every  year agriculture  mainly livestock and rice cultivation  accounts for 40  but fossil fuels and waste including  landfill  are also significant sources the good  news is that existing solutions could  reduce methane emissions from  human activity by 45 by 2030 that's  180 million tons a year  so thomas what are some of those  solutions that you were talking about  there  well you could manage coal mines better  you could detect leaks in gas pipes you  could  reduce food and organic waste going to  landfill you could cut down the amount  of meat you're eating and also  grow rice more sustainably action now in  the 2030s  could reduce the the rise in global  temperatures by 0.3 of a degree  by the 2040s so it's quite significant  and  all this is about keeping that  temperature rise down isn't it yes you  can see the numbers just ticking up the  whole time can't you and it's rare that  we have a win-win when we're talking  about the climate but this  is good for the planet it's good for our  health it's good for the economy  too and remember uh that this is swift  and significant holding down those  temperature rises  one scientist has said if we're talking  about trying to meet those  paris climate gold targets of 1.5  degrees  you need to use every climate trick in  the book and on page one  should be methane emissions thomas thank  you  and on this week's climate cast we're  talking carbon emissions from how much  the amazon rainforest absorbs to the  role of our oceans  in tackling climate change that's  available from tomorrow wherever you  normally get your podcast from  and now in today's other climate news  boris johnson has told a meeting of  ministers from 35 countries that the uk  is doing everything it can  to make the cop 26 climate summit in  november a success  the prime minister said the conference  being held in glasgow  needs to produce concrete action  otherwise there's no chance of keeping  our planet cool  [Applause]  more than a quarter of the world's total  greenhouse emissions came from  china in 2019 exceeding combined  emissions from the developed world  for the first time research from u.s  based think tank the rhodium group  said china was responsible for more than  14 gigatons of co2  that's triple its level in 1990. china  has vowed to bring total emissions down  from 2030 and be carbon neutral by 2060.  [Applause]  the royal botanic gardens queue are  encouraging people to reconnect with  nature  this summer with the secret world of  plants celebrating british biodiversity  it features large-scale immersive  plantscapes  representing contrasting landscapes from  across the uk  from moorlands to sand dunes  conservationists in chile are  reintroducing the rear to its native  home in the patagonia region  where the flightless bird is almost  extinct because of illegal hunting  ria's largely vegetarian diet makes them  an essential part of the ecosystem  helping distribute seeds through the  region last month  14 rears were released into patagonia's  national park by tompkins conservation  who are aiming to get a hundred adults  into the wild  and scientists in the us have found an  eco-friendly way to cut the grass  a group from the university of  california are ditching traditional  lawn mowers and instead they're allowing  sheep to graze on campus lawns  they're hoping using the four-legged  lawn mowers  will lower emissions and increase  biodiversity in the area  now in two weeks time the science  museum's newest exhibition will open  and it's all about carbon capture but  climate  activists are calling for people to  boycott it and that's because  oil company shell is the major sponsor  of the exhibition something  many of those who've contributed to the  exhibits aren't happy about  our climate correspondent lisa holland  reports  [Applause]  london's science museum is preparing to  reopen after lockdown  with the uk's first exhibition on  capturing carbon  the main attraction is a future climate  solution  a full-scale mechanical tree brought  over from america  it replicates how trees naturally soak  up  carbon dioxide it's widely accepted that  technology to capture carbon  and store it will be needed for the uk  to get to net  zero by 2050 but amongst  some contributors like the climate  scientist involved in this display on  capturing carbon in woodlands  the excitement of taking part has been  overshadowed  by learning the exhibition is being  sponsored by the oil company  shell on the one hand i want to support  the exhibition  and the the key messages of the  exhibition but then on the other hand  i don't want to be associated with  sponsorship by  big oil companies the museum has  defended its choice of sponsors  actually we keep complete editorial  control of our exhibitions  no matter the sponsor so  they're actually not no sponsor is ever  involved in the exhibition per se  shells says its target is to become a  net zero emissions energy business by  2050  and it has a long-standing relationship  with the science  museum which is planning to open the  exhibition in two weeks time  lisa holland sky news central london  new research by the royal college of  psychiatrists has found  two-thirds of people in the uk say  climate issues are affecting their  mental health and 84 percent  think it'll have an impact in 10 years  time so how can we care for the planet  and our mental well-being well professor  lorraine whitmarsh is an environmental  psychologist  at the university of bath and joins me  now welcome to you  so just how big a problem do you think  that climate anxiety is  i think it is something which we don't  know very much about to be honest we do  know  that the majority of the public say that  they are very concerned about climate  change  but the percentage that say that it's  something which is  impacting on their quality of life in  significant ways for example not being  able to sleep  or disruption to work is extremely low  um  actually we're seeing that a lot of  people that do express concern about  climate change tend to channel that into  doing something positive  about uh about the environment so  actually it's not always necessarily  a problem i think and to what extent do  you think these are generalized concerns  about the future of the planet  are they more specific people worrying  say that their  local area could flood in the future or  they could be  subject to more droughts  i think what we've found is that  generally it tends to be a rather more  abstract concern people are  worried about um various aspects of  climate change  but in the uk at least it tends to be an  issue which people  think will affect people in other  countries and future generations more  than  themselves personally so i think it is  probably a bit more of an abstract  concern  for the majority of people at the moment  but but clearly  it is an issue so what would your advice  be to anyone who  is worried about the environment who has  climate anxiety  i mean i think people who say that it is  significantly affecting  their mental health obviously have to um  probably seek  help with that i mean i think that there  aren't many people for whom it is a  significant disruption but if that is  causing them a greater anxiety  they can get medical support but i think  what we generally um  advocate is that the more people can um  look into ways in which they can do  their bit to actually help address  climate change the more they can join up  with  like-minded people join groups that are  taking action on climate change  and get social support and also feel  part of a collective effort to take  action  the more they're likely to feel that  they aren't helpless actually in terms  of what climate change  uh the impacts that it's going to have  so i think sort of channeling that  anxiety into positive action  is generally um something that we would  recommend  professor lorraine whitmarsh thanks very  much indeed  now in the latest of our series of  climate diaries we hear the story  of 69 year old saddeman from indonesia  over nearly 25 years of hard work he  succeeded in turning the previously  barren hills around central java  green providing water for local  communities in the process  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  that's all for today on the daily  climate show tomorrow how you can help  save britain's  only spiny mammal under threat because  of our changing climate  you can catch that tomorrow here on sky  news at 6 30 and 9 30 and on our social  channels  thanks for watching see you then  you

